\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces System view. The fence sits between the agent and an irreversible provider. Identity recognition (A) is decided by two exact layers and a calibrated residual judge; fence-level at-most-once commitment (B) is enforced at the durable log under its storage and scheduling assumptions; external exactly-once (C) is not provided, because a crash inside the commit window (dashed) leaves no local record of an accepted effect (Proposition~\ref {prop:amo}).}}{27}{figure.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:architecture}{{2}{27}{System view. The fence sits between the agent and an irreversible provider. Identity recognition (A) is decided by two exact layers and a calibrated residual judge; fence-level at-most-once commitment (B) is enforced at the durable log under its storage and scheduling assumptions; external exactly-once (C) is not provided, because a crash inside the commit window (dashed) leaves no local record of an accepted effect (Proposition~\ref {prop:amo})}{figure.2}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces Decision flow for one request. The certifier and the refuter settle their pairs without a model call; only the residual reaches the judge, and the judge can cause suppression only through the multiplicity-corrected threshold. Every path that is not certified ends in execution, flagged or not.}}{28}{figure.3}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:flow}{{3}{28}{Decision flow for one request. The certifier and the refuter settle their pairs without a model call; only the residual reaches the judge, and the judge can cause suppression only through the multiplicity-corrected threshold. Every path that is not certified ends in execution, flagged or not}{figure.3}{}}
